bea832dcc350995dc0078c23c01659.svg)\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n![](images/eb/ebdd9b24742ae0d3114ff019b2a7c3619fbe9956b995ec762e4c64596f253371.svg)\n\nand the general solution is\n![](images/b5/b58a4f0c0aa22bcf20d95712b435e32e6d83d0abb60190284dd16196eb906742.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087871 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6369e1a3-96fe-518c-bd64-2e83f3ab9685"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087872 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2168103f-a07e-527a-b5ab-cb35206d23bf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088391 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=6{e}^{4x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $6{e}^{4x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $4$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x+4{x}^{2}){e}^{4x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2+16x+16{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2+16x+16{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}-8\\alpha (2x+4{x}^{2}){e}^{4x}+16\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha (2+16x+16{x}^{2}-16x-32{x}^{2}+16{x}^{2}){e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =6{e}^{4x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =6 \\\\ \\alpha  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186086999 :answer-field/correct "answer-aacaccd9-fea2-5f32-9106-999e883a5424" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e51b4573-1df8-5578-8d0c-a6d91ab27a97" "answer-aacaccd9-fea2-5f32-9106-999e883a5424" "answer-fcd0a794-e178-5586-b56f-c91537da2545" "answer-d607fed8-7be8-55fd-b2a7-42278785c59d" "answer-17d07888-62a7-5089-85be-41fe6c4f2fbb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e51b4573-1df8-5578-8d0c-a6d91ab27a97" :answer/id #uuid "e51b4573-1df8-5578-8d0c-a6d91ab27a97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}+2x{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aacaccd9-fea2-5f32-9106-999e883a5424" :answer/id #uuid "aacaccd9-fea2-5f32-9106-999e883a5424" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcd0a794-e178-5586-b56f-c91537da2545" :answer/id #uuid "fcd0a794-e178-5586-b56f-c91537da2545" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+Bx{e}^{4x}-{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d607fed8-7be8-55fd-b2a7-42278785c59d" :answer/id #uuid "d607fed8-7be8-55fd-b2a7-42278785c59d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}+x{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17d07888-62a7-5089-85be-41fe6c4f2fbb" :answer/id #uuid "17d07888-62a7-5089-85be-41fe6c4f2fbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-{x}^{2}{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6369e1a3-96fe-518c-bd64-2e83f3ab9685" :question/id #uuid "6369e1a3-96fe-518c-bd64-2e83f3ab9685" :question/math-academy-id "q-93688" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=8{e}^{5x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8{e}^{5x}.$ In this case, the constant factor in the exponent is $5$, which coincides with the double root of the auxiliary equation. Therefore, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =\\alpha (2x+5{x}^{2}){e}^{5x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\alpha (2+20x+25{x}^{2}){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute ${y}_{p},$ ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and ${y}_{p}^{″}$ into the ODE and solve for $\\alpha :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha (2+20x+25{x}^{2}){e}^{5x}-10\\alpha (2x+5{x}^{2}){e}^{5x}+25\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha (2+20x+25{x}^{2}-20x-50{x}^{2}+25{x}^{2}){e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha  & =8 \\\\ \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2b513567-04b5-522b-9040-43615812c5c7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2b513567-04b5-522b-9040-43615812c5c7" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b513567-04b5-522b-9040-43615812c5c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d885a08e-7617-5674-8bbc-aeee233e5b4f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-13784b02-b4fa-5428-8054-3023ffb7b9cf" "answer-f33ac88d-8b18-5091-bd81-4003268430b2" "answer-16229b38-60cb-5780-8ad0-917d7c6f87c9" "answer-d885a08e-7617-5674-8bbc-aeee233e5b4f" "answer-c60cd13b-e33a-55b0-80ff-3f4f5cdef8ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13784b02-b4fa-5428-8054-3023ffb7b9cf" :answer/id #uuid "13784b02-b4fa-5428-8054-3023ffb7b9cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x}+Bx{e}^{-5x}+2{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f33ac88d-8b18-5091-bd81-4003268430b2" :answer/id #uuid "f33ac88d-8b18-5091-bd81-4003268430b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{5x}-2x{e}^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16229b38-60cb-5780-8ad0-917d7c6f87c9" :answer/id #uuid "16229b38-60cb-5780-8ad0-917d7c6f87c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{5x}-x{e}^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d885a08e-7617-5674-8bbc-aeee233e5b4f" :answer/id #uuid "d885a08e-7617-5674-8bbc-aeee233e5b4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c60cd13b-e33a-55b0-80ff-3f4f5cdef8ab" :answer/id #uuid "c60cd13b-e33a-55b0-80ff-3f4f5cdef8ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+8{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2168103f-a07e-527a-b5ab-cb35206d23bf" :question/id #uuid "2168103f-a07e-527a-b5ab-cb35206d23bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-93839" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{3x},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ by substituting the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =A{e}^{2(0)}+B(0){e}^{2(0)}+{e}^{3(0)} \\\\ 1 & =A+1 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $y$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2A{e}^{2x}+2Bx{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{2x}+3{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=2$ into the derivative we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2A{e}^{2(0)}+2B(0){e}^{2(0)}+B{e}^{2(0)}+3{e}^{3(0)} \\\\ 2 & =2A+B+3 \\\\ -1 & =2A+B \\\\ -1 & =2(0)+B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A=0$ and $B=-1,$ we have the following solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny=-x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a83da9b5-ac88-5cef-b37a-8df85c7f1afb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a83da9b5-ac88-5cef-b37a-8df85c7f1afb" :answer-field/id #uuid "a83da9b5-ac88-5cef-b37a-8df85c7f1afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d6ed557e-1178-5bc6-a68f-dde023f26354" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6ed557e-1178-5bc6-a68f-dde023f26354" "answer-fb927a37-497e-5e94-820c-a17a3e59f215" "answer-449d3531-77be-5f66-9133-bd8cb60e1ed8" "answer-58c93f78-afb5-5ee8-96a0-a85936bf2dd9" "answer-bbad4f38-d737-59f6-83cf-3b35c9da3fdf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6ed557e-1178-5bc6-a68f-dde023f26354" :answer/id #uuid "d6ed557e-1178-5bc6-a68f-dde023f26354" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb927a37-497e-5e94-820c-a17a3e59f215" :answer/id #uuid "fb927a37-497e-5e94-820c-a17a3e59f215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-{e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-449d3531-77be-5f66-9133-bd8cb60e1ed8" :answer/id #uuid "449d3531-77be-5f66-9133-bd8cb60e1ed8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58c93f78-afb5-5ee8-96a0-a85936bf2dd9" :answer/id #uuid "58c93f78-afb5-5ee8-96a0-a85936bf2dd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}-x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbad4f38-d737-59f6-83cf-3b35c9da3fdf" :answer/id #uuid "bbad4f38-d737-59f6-83cf-3b35c9da3fdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y={e}^{3x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088949 :tutorial/content "Given a second-order ODE with exponential forcing $f(x) = ae^{bx},$ if $e^{bx}$ and $xe^{bx}$ are *both* terms in the complementary solution, then neither can be a particular solution. Instead, we assume that a particular solution takes the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{bx}.\n$$\n\nFor example, let's find the solution to the following ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 36y = e ^{6x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the complementary solution by solving the associated homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda -6) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda = 6$ is a repeated root. Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 x} + Bxe^{6 x}.\n$$\n\nSecond, we find a particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE. We see that the right-hand side is $e^{6x},$ and $e^{6x}$ and $xe^{6x}$ are both terms in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x} & =6\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\ \\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =12x\\alpha {e}^{6x}+36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+12\\alpha x{e}^{6x} \\\\  & =36\\alpha {x}^{2}{e}^{6x}+2\\alpha {e}^{6x}+24\\alpha x{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$  we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\underline {36 \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}}\n+2 \\alpha e ^{6x} +\\underline{ 24 \\alpha x e ^{6x} }-12(\\underline{6 \\alpha x^ 2e ^{6x} }+ \n\\underline {2 \\alpha x e^{6x}})\n+\\underline{36  \\alpha x^2 e ^{6x}} = e^{6x}.\n$$\n\nCanceling the underlined terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha {e}^{6x} & ={e}^{6x}\\,⇒\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2} x^2 e^{6x},\n$$\n\nand the general solution is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{c} \\\\  & =A{e}^{6x}+Bx{e}^{6x}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087947 :activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
